## Why This Is Asked
Testing offline sync in PWAs is a core beginner task that exercises service workers, local storage, and synchronization logic in a deterministic way.

## Key Concepts
- Service workers offline caching and background sync
- Local storage/state replicas and reconciliation
- Network emulation in tests and deterministic assertions
- Retry patterns to avoid flaky tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright offline-then-online test outline
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('offline note sync', async ({ browser }) => {
  const context = await browser.newContext();
  const page = await context.newPage();
  await context.setOffline(true);
  await page.goto('https://note.example');
  await page.click('#new-note');
  await page.fill('#title','Offline note');
  await page.fill('#content','content');
  await context.setOffline(false);
  await page.waitForResponse(resp => resp.url().includes('/notes') && resp.status() === 200);
  // verify sync and UI badge
  await expect(page.locator('#status')).toHaveText(/Synced/);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multi-device sync?
- How would you detect and handle merge conflicts during offline edits?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design realistic cross-service end-to-end tests with real-time updates, failure modes, and proper data isolation.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time event streams (WebSocket/SSE) and state machine validation
- Test isolation with per-tenant data and fresh environments
- Deterministic inputs via seed data and mocked external services
- Idempotency and eventual consistency verification across services
- Failure injection testing (disconnects, latency, partial outages)

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch using Playwright
const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test.describe('E2E Food Delivery Flow', () => {
  let wsServer, testData;

  test.beforeAll(async () => {
    wsServer = await startMockWebSocketServer();
    testData = await seedDeterministicTestData();
  });

  test('complete delivery flow with driver tracking', async ({ page }) => {
    const driverUpdates = [];

    // Simulate real-time driver location stream
    wsServer.on('driver-location', (data) => driverUpdates.push(data));

    // 1. Place order
    await page.goto('/order');
    await page.fill('#address', testData.address);
    await page.click('#place-order');
    const orderId = await expectOrderConfirmed(page);

    // 2. Restaurant accepts
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'accepted' });
    await expectOrderStatus(page, 'accepted');

    // 3. Driver assigned
    await wsServer.emit('driver-assigned', { orderId, driver: testData.driver });
    await expectDriverAssigned(page, testData.driver);

    // 4. Verify location/ETA streaming
    await wsServer.emit('driver-location', { lat: 40.7128, lng: -74.0060, eta: 10 });
    await expectETA(page, '10 min');

    // 5. Payment processed
    await stubPaymentAPI({ orderId, status: 'success' });
    await expectPaymentStatus(page, 'paid');

    // 6. Delivery completes
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'delivered' });
    await expectOrderStatus(page, 'delivered');

    // Verify idempotent event handling
    await wsServer.emit('order-status', { orderId, status: 'delivered' });
    expect(driverUpdates).toHaveLength(1); // No duplicate processing

    // Verify eventual consistency
    await expectExternalServiceSynced(orderId);
  });

  test('handles driver disconnect gracefully', async ({ page }) => {
    const orderId = await placeOrder(page);
    await wsServer.simulateDisconnect();

    await expectReconnectionLogic(page);
    await expectDriverReassigned(page);
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for optimistic UI and offline resilience are common in messaging apps. This question probes ability to design deterministic E2E tests that cover front-end and back-end coordination, error paths, and data isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Optimistic UI updates
- Network fault simulation and retry handling
- Test data isolation and deterministic seeding
- Front-end/back-end state reconciliation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright test snippet (simplified)
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('delete with optimistic update and Undo', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://chat.app');
  await page.fill('#newMessage', 'hello world');
  await page.click('#send');
  const msg = page.locator('.message').last();
  await expect(msg).toHaveText('hello world');

  await msg.click('.delete');
  await expect(page.locator('.toast-undo')).toBeVisible();
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).not.toContainText('hello world');

  await page.click('.toast-undo');
  await expect(page.locator('.message')).toContainText('hello world');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to handle multiple concurrent deletions in a single channel?
- How would you verify backend state during intermittent connectivity without flaky tests?

